---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectTrail from '../../components/ProjectTrail.astro';
import { doppel } from '../../data/metrics';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';
import '../../styles/project.css';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'doppel')!;
---
<Base
  title="Doppel.exe: who's at the keyboard? | Abtin Aryanpour"
  description="A Windows continuous-authentication agent that checks who is at the keyboard from typing rhythm alone, without storing what they type."
  current="work"
>
  <article class="proj">
    <header class="top">
      <div class="top-sky"><ProjectTrail start={p.start} end={p.end} /></div>
      <div class="top-text wrap">
        <h1 class="display">Doppel.exe</h1>
        <p class="lead">A Windows agent that keeps checking who’s at the keyboard from their typing rhythm, without ever storing what they type.</p>
        <p class="status">Finished in October 2026. <a href="https://github.com/abtinmbm/doppel">Code on GitHub</a></p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="rec-sec wrap" aria-labelledby="rec-h">
      <h2 id="rec-h" class="visually-hidden">What it stores</h2>
      <figure class="rec">
        <p class="rec-line num" aria-hidden="true"><span>[false, 2,</span> <span>122.0, 35.0, −87.0]</span></p>
        <dl class="rec-key small">
          <div><dt class="num">false, 2</dt><dd>A rough label for the pair of keys: they’re on opposite halves of the keyboard, 1.5 to 3.5 key widths apart. Not which keys.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="num">122.0</dt><dd>How long the first key was held, in milliseconds.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="num">35.0</dt><dd>From pressing the first key to pressing the second.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="num">−87.0</dt><dd>From releasing the first key to pressing the second. It’s negative because the keys overlapped.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <figcaption class="small">A record as Doppel stores it. That’s everything it keeps about a pair of keys. Space, Shift and other keys get a label by kind instead, like <span class="num">{doppel.secondRecord}</span>.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="why wrap" aria-labelledby="why-h">
      <h2 id="why-h" class="visually-hidden">Why it exists</h2>
      <p class="statement">I leave my PC on at home. My brother doesn’t hack anything. He just waits until I walk away and uses the session I left logged in.</p>
      <p class="why-more">Locking after a few idle minutes doesn’t help, because he sits down sooner. A password checks you once. Doppel keeps checking, and when the person typing stops typing like me, it calls Windows’ own lock.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="how wrap grid" aria-labelledby="how-h">
      <h2 id="how-h">How it decides</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><p><b>Collect.</b> A keyboard hook times every key press and release, and turns each pair of keys into the record above.</p></li>
        <li><p><b>Label without letters.</b> Two letters get a label from keyboard geometry. Other keys get their kind, such as Space. Key identities exist only in memory while a key is held.</p></li>
        <li><p><b>Store encrypted.</b> Records are shuffled in batches of {doppel.batch}, so word lengths can’t be read back, then encrypted with AES-GCM. The key is protected by Windows DPAPI, and nothing leaves the PC.</p></li>
        <li><p><b>Score.</b> A window of 100 records is compared with my profile on a log scale, with each deviation capped, so one long pause can’t swamp it.</p></li>
        <li><p><b>Decide.</b> The score becomes a trust value from 0 to 1. Several low values in a row lock the screen, and after a quiet spell the first low value does.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="results wrap grid" aria-labelledby="results-h">
      <h2 id="results-h">I checked the method on public data first</h2>
      <div class="chart">
        <table class="facts-table">
          <caption class="small">Public datasets, comparing methods. These aren’t the accuracy of the live app.</caption>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Reproduced a published benchmark</th><td>Within <span class="num">{doppel.cmuGap}</span> mean equal error rate of the CMU results, across 3 detectors</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Privacy label against real letters</th><td><span class="num">{doppel.labelEer}</span> error with the label that stores no letters, against <span class="num">{doppel.lettersEer}</span> storing real letter pairs</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Fixing outliers</th><td>Mean error rate from <span class="num">{doppel.eerBefore}</span> to <span class="num">{doppel.eerAfter}</span>, about 7 times lower, confirmed on {doppel.heldOutUsers} held-out users</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="notes">
        <p>The equal error rate is where wrongly letting someone in and wrongly locking me out happen equally often. Lower is better.</p>
        <p>The label that stores no letters did better than storing the real letters. Letter pairs split Space and Shift into groups too small to learn from.</p>
        <p>Thinking pauses were swamping whole windows. Measuring timings on a log scale and capping each deviation fixed it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story wrap" aria-labelledby="short-h">
      <div class="problem">
        <h2 id="short-h">Where it stopped</h2>
        <p>The typing pipeline works end to end, as a dry run that says when it would lock. The lock limits were never tuned on enough of my own typing: I collected {doppel.ownerRecords} records of the {doppel.ownerPlanned} I planned. So I make no accuracy claim for the live app. Writing style, attack tests and a mouse signal were planned, then dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="problem">
        <h3>Known gaps</h3>
        <p>Someone at the session can end the process. Reading or scrolling without typing is invisible to it. Anything done before the lock gets through.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="next wrap" aria-label="Stack">
      <p class="small dim">Built with Python, NumPy, pandas, SQLite, AES-GCM, Windows DPAPI and Windows APIs.</p>
    </section>

    <nav class="onward wrap" aria-label="Next project">
      <a class="display" href="/projects/andon/">Next project: Andon</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .rec-sec { padding-top: var(--s8); }
  .rec { margin: 0; }
  .rec-line { font-size: clamp(30px, 6vw, 88px); line-height: 1.05; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .rec-line span { white-space: nowrap; }
  .rec-key { margin: var(--s6) 0 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--gap); }
  .rec-key div { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding-top: var(--s3); display: grid; gap: var(--s2); align-content: start; }
  .rec-key dt { font-weight: 500; }
  .rec-key dd { margin: 0; color: var(--dim); }
  .rec figcaption { margin-top: var(--s5); color: var(--dim); max-width: 64ch; }
  .story h2 { margin-bottom: var(--s3); }
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .rec-key { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; row-gap: var(--s5); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 599px) {
    .rec-key { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
